The Web-based Centralized Faculty Evaluation System was developed to make the faculty evaluation process easier to manage. The system keeps the evaluation information in one place so that the records can be accessed and managed without having to check different files. MySQL is used to store the data, while Node.js and Express.js are used for the backend. The frontend uses Next.js, React, TypeScript, Vite, and Tailwind CSS. These parts work together to allow the users to log in, complete evaluations, and manage the records based on their assigned role.
The data in the system is stored in different tables. The User table contains the account details and role of each user. The system has three main types of users: Admin, School Head/Principal, and Student. The Faculty records contain the information of the teachers and faculty members included in the evaluation. The evaluation questions are stored in the Evaluation Form, while the answers submitted by the users are stored as Evaluation Responses. The system also keeps the School Year and Semester information so that the evaluations can be associated with the correct academic period. These records are connected in the database so that the information can be used by the different parts of the system.
Each user has different functions after logging in. The Admin can create School Head/Principal and Student accounts, add faculty records, manage the evaluation forms, set the school year and semester, and view reports. The School Head/Principal can evaluate faculty members, submit evaluations, and view their evaluation history. Students can log in, evaluate their teachers, and submit their evaluations. The system checks the user's account and role before allowing access to these functions. This prevents users from opening features that are not part of their assigned role.
When a user submits an evaluation, the information is sent from the frontend to the backend through the REST API. The backend receives the information and checks the submitted data before saving it to the MySQL database. This helps prevent incomplete information from being saved. The stored responses can then be accessed by the authorized Admin when viewing records and reports. The system also uses Create, Read, Update, and Delete (CRUD) operations for the records that need to be added, viewed, changed, or removed. Since the evaluation records are kept in one database, they can be managed more easily instead of being collected from separate forms.
During the development of the system, the researchers use Visual Studio Code for writing the code, EchoAPI for testing the API requests, and GitHub for storing the project files and keeping track of changes. For the current deployment, the frontend is deployed on Vercel, while the backend and MySQL database are deployed on Railway. This setup allows the frontend to communicate with the backend and database through the internet. The system is still being tested and improved, so changes may still be made to the system as the researchers continue the development process.
